.TH CARDEA\-TEAM 1 "2026" "cardea-kernel" "Cardea"
.SH NAME
cardea team \- run, watch and change an agent dev team on this host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cardea team
.I command
[\fIarguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cardea team
is the team kit's command line, a subcommand group of
.BR cardea (1).
It acts as the team's supervisor. The supervisor's identity is bound to the
.B cardea-kernel
account, so the command runs its work as that account through
.BR sudo (8):
a narrow rule in
.I /etc/sudoers.d/cardea-team
lets members of the group
.B cardea-operators
run one fixed program,
.IR /usr/libexec/cardea-team ,
as
.BR cardea-kernel ,
with no arguments (the request is sent on its standard input).
You do not type
.B sudo
yourself. Add a person to the group with
.BR "adduser NAME cardea-operators" ;
the membership lets that person act as the team's supervisor.
.PP
Nothing in the command decides what is allowed. Each read and write goes to the kernel as the
supervisor, and the kernel checks the supervisor's live authority, as it does for the dashboard.
The code run is the code of the installed kernel (\fI/usr/lib/cardea/teamkit\fR, beside
\fI/usr/lib/cardea/cardea\fR), not a source checkout. Output never includes a credential.
.PP
The host file is
.IR /etc/cardea/team.host.json .
It must be readable by
.BR cardea-kernel .
Its
.B provisioning.out
should be in a directory that account can write, for example
.IR /var/lib/cardea/team/provision.json ,
and the host file itself writable by that account for
.BR set .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
The team's members and their runs, work by state, mail, approvals, spend against its ceilings,
token expiry dates and the last failed runs. While the team is paused: why, and the failures
recorded then.
.TP
.BR pause " [\fIreason\fR]"
The kill switch. Provisions the supervisor disabled, so the kernel cancels every unfinished run
and nothing is admitted until
.BR resume .
The reason is one plain line, kept for
.BR status ;
never put a secret in it.
.TP
.B resume
Provisions the supervisor enabled; triggers admit again.
.TP
.B stop
Pauses the team and deletes its agent group; the collections and records stay. Asks for
confirmation first.
.TP
.B apply
Puts the work and decisions collections and the agent group to the kernel.
.TP
.BR render " [\fB\-\-out\fR \fIDIR\fR]"
Shows the documents the team is made of, for review, or writes them to
.I DIR
(private files, written as you).
.TP
.BR provision " [\fB\-\-paused\fR]"
Merges the team's principals and grants into the base provisioning document and provisions it.
.B \-\-paused
writes the supervisor disabled.
.TP
.B settings
The settings in force in plain words: run and spend limits, schedules, email limit,
developers, model, and the size of the product goals. Credentials are not shown.
.TP
.BR set " \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]"
Changes one setting. It shows what would change and a confirmation prompt, then applies exactly
what was shown (render, provision, apply, and an audit line). The checks are those of the
dashboard's Settings page.
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops after the preview;
.B \-\-yes
skips the prompt. Keys:
.RS
.TP
.B runs.team, runs.\fIrole\fR
Runs a day (role: pm, tl, dev or pe).
.TP
.B usd.team, usd.per_run, usd.\fIrole\fR
Dollars a day, and the most one run may cost.
.TP
.B schedule.\fIrole\fR
Cron schedules separated by ';', or 'none'.
.TP
.B email.rate_limit
Emails to the client, COUNT/DAYS, for example 10/1.
.TP
.B developers
How many developers.
.TP
.B model
The model name.
.TP
.B goals, non_goals
The product's goals. A value of '@FILE' reads that file as you; '\-' reads standard input.
.RE
.TP
.BR mail " \fItarget\fR \fItext\fR"
Sends a message as the supervisor. The target is
.BR tl ,
.BR pm ,
.BR pe ,
.B ux
(that role's pool) or
.B dev.N
(developer N, counting from dev.0). Put
.B \-\-
before a text that starts with a dash.
.TP
.BR "mail list" " [\fB\-\-member\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-item\fR \fICOLLECTION/ID\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-after\fR \fICURSOR\fR]"
Reads the team's mail, newest first, with full text. The text is the team's: control characters
in it are replaced before it is shown.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when a command is refused or fails (the reason is printed), or a confirmation is
declined; 2 on a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cardea/team.host.json
The host file (see the team kit's example).
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/cardea-team
The sudoers rule.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/cardea-team
The helper that runs as cardea-kernel.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cardea (1),
.BR sudoers (5)
